lines+=section(9,'Objective-by-objective findings','All 15 concept/objective pairs are recorded below with actual labels (the code itself where a descriptive label is absent). A coverage; B robustness; C calibration; D calculation/integration; E graph-use; F upper synthesis; G no material gap. These immutable BEFORE findings preceded content authoring. Subsequent checks also identified the need for explicit initial equilibrium/zero-supply-offset assumptions in three expectations items.')
lines+=table(['Concept / objective — label','Count','BEFORE classes','Finding','Content changes'],[(x['concept']+' / '+x['objective']+' — '+x['label'],x['count'],','.join(x['classifications']),x['finding'],sum(z['action']=='content-revised' and z['concept']==x['concept'] and z['objective']==x['objective'] for z in actions)) for x in obj])
lines+=section(10,'Coverage gaps','No selected objective was materially absent; no A gap or new record was justified. Rational expectations lacked the name, but its selected credibility/information application already existed. Clarifying that framework in an existing Hard question does not add a new objective. Absent advanced extensions are boundaries.')
lines+=section(11,'Robustness gaps','Some Hard SRPC graph items merely duplicated easier coordinate comparisons. Two are strengthened with a supplied demand determinant, curve classification, percentage-point differences and the natural-rate distinction. Repeated upper expectations and single-arithmetic forms receive selective inverse/conditional tasks. Simpler retained variants and repeated feedback remain disclosed.')
lines+=section(12,'Calibration gaps','No whole-selection C gap was found: Hard already contains expansion/disinflation graph sequences, expectation adjustment, credibility constraints, structural unemployment and cumulative sacrifice arithmetic. Eight canonical-Hard records are revised for precision or stronger application; none changes tier. The two ordinary graph improvements address local robustness, not a claim that normal exam reasoning was absent from the bank.')
lines+=section(13,'Calculation/integration gaps','The numerical review covers all 37 sacrifice-ratio records containing numerical information, including unchanged calculations, denominators, unit interpretations and a growth-versus-level distinction. Their underlying arithmetic was sound; some assumptions and upper-tier demand needed improvement. Six additional revised graph/natural-rate calculations are independently checked, giving 43 numerical/measurement validation entries in total; all 14 revised quantitative items are included.','Derivations use final-stem inputs and exact fractions or independently transcribed image coordinates, then compare with answers. Hash checks are separate structural checks. Constant potential output, positive loss magnitudes, annual versus cumulative windows and percentage points are explicitly distinguished where needed.')
lines+=section(14,'SRPC findings','With expectations, supply conditions and natural unemployment fixed, demand expansion moves up-left along SRPC; contraction reverses that movement. Supplied demand events are transferred into Phillips reasoning without reopening monetary mechanics. New Hard applications distinguish actual unemployment from the unchanged natural rate and use both axis changes.')
lines+=section(15,'LRPC findings','The vertical LRPC is correctly located at natural unemployment. Demand surprises do not permanently move it; labor-market matching changes can. The accelerationist correction distinguishes a constant required surprise from an ever-higher inflation rate under last-period adaptive expectations. Prompts predicting a return to natural now state when repeated efforts to maintain the below-natural gap stop.')
lines+=section(16,'Expectations findings','Actual inflation alone does not identify expected inflation. Two graph prompts now exclude supply/natural-rate shifts before attributing a curve shift to expectations. Hard 263/264 specify an initial equality of actual and expected inflation before concluding that unemployment crosses the natural rate; Hard 246 explicitly excludes a supply offset.','PG5-PC-L-006 uses B=(6,6), a stated Phillips equation and a measured +1.1 supply offset to infer expected inflation of 5.5. Without that independent measurement, only expected inflation plus the supply offset is identified. The graph does not supply a unique causal diagnosis on its own.')
lines+=section(17,'Adaptive and rational expectations','Adaptive tasks use observed inflation or forecast errors; the new numerical graph continuations explicitly specify one-period updating rather than treating it as a universal adjustment speed. They compare holding unemployment fixed with holding inflation fixed, or impose a later no-deflation constraint.','The revised Hard credibility item states that rational expectations use available information, including credible policy announcements. Forecast errors can occur with new information, and nominal contracts can still delay adjustment. Neither rational expectations nor an announcement alone guarantees perfect forecasting or zero output cost.')
lines+=section(18,'Disinflation findings','The existing Hard b-to-d-to-c sequence already distinguishes movement along SRPC2 from a subsequent downward SRPC shift. The revised Legendary comparison contrasts contracts that retain old expectations with immediate expectations/contract adjustment, making the different transition paths conditional. Another upper item requires a forecast of natural-rate return at fixed inflation to be reconciled with an appropriate lower SRPC. Disinflation remains slower price growth, not necessarily falling prices.')
lines+=section(19,'Sacrifice-ratio findings','The course convention is cumulative output loss relative to potential, expressed as a percentage of an annual output benchmark or a sum of annual percentage shortfalls, divided by the percentage-point inflation reduction. Dollar-loss-per-point tasks remain explicitly dollar-valued, not dimensionless ratios. Different denominators and annual benchmarks are not mixed.','New upper tasks reconcile omitted earlier losses, compare total versus per-point cost, solve a remaining-stage loss ceiling, and infer stage-specific ratios from the combined ratio. Unsupported judgments such as “not extreme” are removed in favor of explicit comparisons. No valid legacy numerical key was reversed solely to increase revisions.')
lines+=section(20,'AD-AS to Phillips integration','Demand-driven output gains map to lower cyclical unemployment and inflation pressure along a fixed SRPC; adverse supply can raise both inflation and unemployment and shift the relationship. The supply-shock feedback correction now describes an initial adverse shift followed by expectations reinforcement, not the disappearance of a demand-created unemployment gain. All underlying AD-AS and fiscal definitions remain unchanged.')
lines+=section(21,'Supply-shock findings','The bank already distinguishes stagflation from movement along a fixed downward SRPC. An upper revision separates a temporary supply offset that disappears from higher expectations that persist after contracts reset. The final curve is above the original, while its position relative to the disruption-period curve needs the relative magnitudes; no unique explanation is inferred from inflation alone.')
lines+=section(22,'Natural-rate findings','The revised matching-reform item separates a 0.8-point structural natural-rate decline from a 0.7-point cyclical gap below the new rate. Long-run unemployment returns to 5.6 percent after the demand surprise ends. It uses an original matching-reform task, not the exam’s demographic scenario. Cyclical recession is not treated as an automatic permanent LRPC shift.')
lines+=section(23,'Graph-use findings','All six distinct image files across 11 selected registrations were visually inspected against current file hashes. Symbolic lrpc.webp and srpc.webp have correct point mappings and detailed descriptions; their metadata was preserved. SRPC-01 through SRPC-04 receive fuller axes, line-coordinate, point and guide evidence. No image byte changed or new graph was created.','All 66 graph-linked records render, enlarge and deliver descriptions in the generated quest. Trial by Graph remains at 24 eligible records: 4 Easy, 8 Medium, 4 Hard, 4 Elite and 4 Legendary. Forty-two graph-linked records retain their existing non-required flags; repairing delivery does not silently expand eligibility.')
lines+=section(24,'Disposition of all 42 known failures','Every prior failure is fixed using its existing per-concept registered asset. No record was dismissed as not actually broken; none remains unresolved. Each final file hash matches the original image bytes and each browser check passes. Full source/final references and tests appear in `path-repair-validation.json`.')
lines+=table(['Question ID','Registered final image','Disposition'],[(x['id'],x['finalImage'],'Fixed; image, enlargement, description pass') for x in paths])
lines+=section(25,'Upper-tier synthesis findings','Selective revisions add: constant-surprise versus rising-inflation reasoning; structural/cyclical decomposition; graph-based reverse expectations with a measured supply offset; multi-period adaptive constraints; temporary supply reversal; two credibility/contract paths; cumulative-window reconciliation; remaining-stage ceilings; and stage-ratio decomposition.','Some retained upper items remain direct, including PG5-PC-X-011, ECON-SP-ELITE-348 and PM2A-SRPC-LB-013. PM2A-SAC-LB-025 remains forward multi-year arithmetic despite clarified assumptions. A mature audit need not replace every usable variant, and this report does not claim uniformly deep upper tiers.')
lines+=section(26,'Strong existing Hard questions retained',f'{len(hard)} canonical-Hard records retain their question content. The complete list is in `retained-hard.json`; some graph records receive evidence/path changes only.')
